      "text": "PFHxS showed the most negative impact, followed by PFOS and PFNA. It is possible that some organic molecules contained in NOM have similar properties or target the same adsorption sites as PFAS. To further investigate the NOM impact mechanisms, Figure\u00a0 4I presents the impact of NOM in the presence of CaSO 4 when NOM is preloaded onto GAC. For all PFAS, the percent qe difference shifted more negative compared to NOM co- loading with CaSO 4 presence (Figure\u00a0 4H). The increased negative im - pact on all PFAS with NOM preloading can be attributed to NOM pore blockage, preventing the access of PFAS molecules (such as PFHxA, PFBS, and GenX) to otherwise available ad - sorption sites. Coexistence of both direct competition and pore- blockage mechanisms has been previously reported (Corwin and Summers\u00a0 2010; Yu et\u00a0al. 2012) and is not surprising, consid- ering the wide range of molecular size and properties of organic compounds in the NOM mixture. Therefore, when evaluating the NOM impacts on PFAS treatment efficacy, it is important to consider the properties of the target PFAS and the source water NOM as well as other source water quality conditions. The NOM isotherms in Figure\u00a05 further investigate NOM interac- tions with GAC, PFAS, and CaSO4. PFAS co- adsorption and NOM"
