      "text": "adsorptive surface to help optimize PFAS treatment efficacy and minimize operational costs. 1 | Introduction With the United States Environmental Protection Agency final - izing a regulation that includes enforceable maximum contam - inant levels or hazard indexes for six per- and polyfluoroalkyl substances (PFAS) (USEPA\u00a0 2024), the demand for effective PFAS water treatment technologies has never been more press - ing. Granular activated carbon (GAC) adsorption is being con - sidered for PFAS treatment because it has been widely used in the water industry, has a demonstrated ability to remove multi - ple PFAS and various co- contaminants, and could be retrofitted in existing conventional filters (AWWA\u00a0 2020). However, source water characteristics can significantly impact GAC treatment efficacy, which can vary widely in practice. Understanding the impact of this variability is important for developing effective and cost- efficient GAC treatment systems, but it can still require case study and optimization for each site. Optimization based on these source water characteristics could be improved by inclu - sion of their effects in mechanistic models, which are currently not available. Multiple factors affect GAC, including GAC and target adsor - bate properties, source water quality, and GAC treatment system operating conditions (Chowdhury et\u00a0al. 2013). Therefore, GAC adsorber design requires conducting adsorption tests under conditions that represent treatment site conditions. Pilot- scale"
